le-loop PID explodes under abrupt load shifts, and how cascaded loops tame the physical world."}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-12","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-12 gap-8 lg:gap-12 items-start","children":[["$","aside",null,{"className":"lg:col-span-3 lg:sticky lg:top-24 space-y-8 font-mono text-xs border-b lg:border-b-0 lg:border-r border-ink-dark/10 dark:border-paper-100/10 pb-8 lg:pb-0 pr-0 lg:pr-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-[10px] text-accent font-bold uppercase tracking-widest","children":"CHAPTER OUTLINE"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-2 text-ink-dark/80 dark:text-paper-200/80 text-[11px]","children":[["$","li","the-delusion-of-single-loop",{"className":"hover:text-accent transition-colors","children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#the-delusion-of-single-loop","className":"block leading-snug","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent font-mono mr-1.5","children":["0",1,"."]}],"I. The Delusion of Single-Loop PID"]}]}],["$","li","the-inner-and-outer-ring",{"className":"hover:text-accent transition-colors","children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#the-inner-and-outer-ring","className":"block leading-snug","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent font-mono mr-1.5","children":["0",2,"."]}],"II. The Inner and Outer Ring"]}]}],["$","li","packaging-v2-5-0",{"className":"hover:text-accent transition-colors","children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#packaging-v2-5-0","className":"block leading-snug","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent font-mono mr-1.5","children":["0",3,"."]}],"III. Packaging for PyPI (v2.5.0)"]}]}],["$","li","what-the-benchmarks-revealed",{"className":"hover:text-accent transition-colors","children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#what-the-benchmarks-revealed","className":"block leading-snug","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent font-mono mr-1.5","children":["0",4,"."]}],"IV. What the Benchmarks Revealed"]}]}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-2 pt-4 border-t border-ink-dark/10 dark:border-paper-100/10 text-[10px] text-ink-muted","children":[["$","div",null,{"children":["AUTHOR: ","Matthew Chen"," (","陳軍宇",")"]}],["$","div",null,{"children":["LOCATION: ","Jiuru / Tamsui, Taiwan"]}],["$","div",null,{"children":["MOOD: ",["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent font-bold uppercase","children":"Analytical"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"pt-2","children":["$","a",null,{"href":"https://github.com/Ch3nOff","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","className":"underline hover:text-accent","children":"GitHub @Ch3nOff"}]}]]}]]}],["$","main",null,{"className":"lg:col-span-6 space-y-12","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"drop-cap font-sans text-lg sm:text-xl text-ink-dark dark:text-paper-100 leading-relaxed font-light","children":"Nearly every beginner who tries to build an inverted pendulum, a gimbal stabilizer, or an autonomous wheeled chassis falls into the same trap: they slap a generic PID formula into their code, spend four weekends turning potentiometers, and end up with a system that either crawls like molasses or oscillates into self-destruction."}],[["$","section","0",{"id":"the-delusion-of-single-loop","className":"space-y-6 pt-4 scroll-mt-28","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink-dark dark:text-paper-50 tracking-tight border-b border-ink-dark/15 dark:border-paper-100/15 pb-2","children":"I. The Delusion of Single-Loop PID"}],"$undefined",[["$","p","0",{"className":"font-sans text-base sm:text-lg text-ink-dark/90 dark:text-paper-100/90 leading-relaxed font-normal","children":"A standard PID controller views the world as a scalar error term: target position minus actual position. But physical motors don't produce position. Motors produce current, which produces torque, which creates angular acceleration, which integrates over time into velocity, which integrates into position."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"font-sans text-base sm:text-lg text-ink-dark/90 dark:text-paper-100/90 leading-relaxed font-normal","children":"Asking a single PID loop to bridge the gap across two integration stages while dealing with friction, backlash, and battery voltage drop is asking for mathematical instability."}]],"$undefined","$undefined","$undefined",["$","div",null,{"className":"lg:hidden p-3 bg-accent/5 border-l-2 border-accent font-hand text-accent text-sm","children":["Note: ","Physics has two integration stages. Your controller needs two loops to match."]}]]}],["$","section","1",{"id":"the-inner-and-outer-ring","className":"space-y-6 pt-4 scroll-mt-28","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink-dark dark:text-paper-50 tracking-tight border-b border-ink-dark/15 dark:border-paper-100/15 pb-2","children":"II. The Inner and Outer Ring"}],"$undefined",[["$","p","0",{"className":"font-sans text-base sm:text-lg text-ink-dark/90 dark:text-paper-100/90 leading-relaxed font-normal","children":"The solution is classic cascaded control: the outer loop calculates the desired velocity needed to close the position gap, and the inner loop runs at 5x to 10x the frequency to ensure the motor reaches that exact velocity immediately."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"font-sans text-base sm:text-lg text-ink-dark/90 dark:text-paper-100/90 leading-relaxed font-normal","children":"The inner loop eats disturbances before the outer loop even realizes friction occurred. If someone pushes the actuator arm, the velocity loop counters the torque spike within 500 microseconds."}]],"$undefined",["$","div",null,{"className":"my-6 p-4 border border-ink-dark/20 dark:border-paper-100/20 bg-paper-100/60 dark:bg-paper-900/60 font-mono text-[10px] sm:text-xs overflow-x-auto text-ink-dark dark:text-paper-100","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"text-[9px] text-accent uppercase tracking-widest pb-2 mb-2 border-b border-ink-dark/10","children":"Cascaded Dual-Loop Topology"}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"leading-tight","children":"[Target Position] ──► [Outer PID: Position] ──► (Target Velocity)\n                                                    │\n                                                    ▼\n [Actual Velocity] ──────────────────────────► [Inner PID: Velocity]\n                                                    │\n                                                    ▼\n                                              [PWM / Torque Out]"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"my-6 border border-ink-dark/20 dark:border-paper-100/20 bg-ink-pure text-paper-100 p-4 font-mono text-xs overflow-x-auto","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex justify-between items-center text-[9px] text-paper-200/60 pb-2 mb-2 border-b border-paper-100/10 uppercase tracking-widest","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":["LANGUAGE: ","python"]}],["$","span",null,{"children":"CH3NOFF KERNEL"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":"# How dual-loop-controller orchestrates cascaded feedback\nfrom dual_loop_controller import DualLoopPID, LoopConfig\n\nconfig = LoopConfig(\n    pos_kp=1.8, pos_ki=0.02, pos_kd=0.15,\n    vel_kp=0.45, vel_ki=0.12, vel_kd=0.01,\n    max_velocity=1200.0, # saturation limit\n    anti_windup=True\n)\n\ncontroller = DualLoopPID(config)\n\n# Fast execution tick (e.g. 500Hz)\ncontrol_output = controller.step(target_pos=90.0, current_pos=sensor_pos, current_vel=sensor_vel, dt=0.002)"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"text-[9px] text-paper-200/50 pt-2 mt-2 border-t border-paper-100/10 italic","children":["// ","Usage example of dual-loop-controller v2.5.0 available on PyPI"]}]]}],"$undefined"]}],["$","section","2",{"id":"packaging-v2-5-0","className":"space-y-6 pt-4 scroll-mt-28","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink-dark dark:text-paper-50 tracking-tight border-b border-ink-dark/15 dark:border-paper-100/15 pb-2","children":"III. Packaging for PyPI (v2.5.0)"}],"$undefined",[["$","p","0",{"className":"font-sans text-base sm:text-lg text-ink-dark/90 dark:text-paper-100/90 leading-relaxed font-normal","children":"Publishing `dual-loop-controller` to PyPI under `chenoff` was an exercise in stripping away cruft. I wanted zero mandatory third-party dependencies so that an embedded engineer running MicroPython on an ESP32 or a student running a Raspberry Pi could simply `pip install dual-loop-controller` and run."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"font-sans text-base sm:text-lg text-ink-dark/90 dark:text-paper-100/90 leading-relaxed font-normal","children":"Version 2.5.0 introduced strict clamping on integral windup, derivative filtering to suppress encoder discretization noise, and a state recorder that can export raw telemetry directly into JSON for offline plotting."}]],["$","figure",null,{"className":"my-8 py-6 border-y-2 border-accent/40 px-4 sm:px-6 bg-accent/5 dark:bg-accent/10","children":["$","blockquote",null,{"className":"font-serif italic text-xl sm:text-2xl text-ink-dark dark:text-paper-50 leading-snug","children":["“","Good libraries don't add features; they eliminate friction between the programmer's intent and physical reality.","”"]}]}],"$undefined","$undefined",["$","div",null,{"className":"lg:hidden p-3 bg-accent/5 border-l-2 border-accent font-hand text-accent text-sm","children":["Note: ","Dependencies are technical debt before they are even written."]}]]}],["$","section","3",{"id":"what-the-benchmarks-revealed","className":"space-y-6 pt-4 scroll-mt-28","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink-dark dark:text-paper-50 tracking-tight border-b border-ink-dark/15 dark:border-paper-100/15 pb-2","children":"IV. What the Benchmarks Revealed"}],"$undefined",[["$","p","0",{"className":"font-sans text-base sm:text-lg text-ink-dark/90 dark:text-paper-100/90 leading-relaxed font-normal","children":"Settling time dropped by 64% compared to a tuned single PID loop under step disturbance. Overshoot was virtually eliminated. Most importantly, the motor no longer makes that horrifying high-pitch whine caused by derivative noise amplification."}]],"$undefined","$undefined","$undefined","$undefined"]}]],["$","footer",null,{"className":"pt-12 border-t border-ink-dark/20 dark:border-paper-100/20 space-y-4 font-mono text-xs","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"text-[10px] text-accent uppercase tracking-widest font-bold","children":"[FOOTNOTES & FORMAL CITATIONS]"}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"space-y-2 list-decimal list-inside text-ink-muted text-[11px]","children":[["$","li","1",{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":["$","span",null,{"className":"text-ink-dark dark:text-paper-100 font-sans","children":"Dual-loop controllers decouple inertial lag from actuator torque, preventing integral windup during non-linear mechanical friction."}]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":["$","span",null,{"className":"text-ink-dark dark:text-paper-100 font-sans","children":"Package dual-loop-controller is hosted on PyPI under username chenoff, providing zero-dependency pure Python and optimized array paths."}]}]]}]]}]]}],["$","aside",null,{"className":"hidden lg:block lg:col-span-3 space-y-8 font-mono text-xs border-l border-ink-dark/10 dark:border-paper-100/10 pl-6","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-[10px] text-accent font-bold uppercase tracking-widest block","children":"MARGINALIA & NOTES"}],[["$","div","0",{"className":"p-3 bg-paper-100/50 dark:bg-paper-900/50 border border-ink-dark/10 dark:border-paper-100/10 space-y-1 relative","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-[8px] text-accent uppercase tracking-widest block","children":["ANNOTATION § 0",1]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"margin-hand text-base text-accent","children":["“","Physics has two integration stages. Your controller needs two loops to match.","”"]}]]}],null,["$","div","2",{"className":"p-3 bg-paper-100/50 dark:bg-paper-900/50 border border-ink-dark/10 dark:border-paper-100/10 space-y-1 relative","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-[8px] text-accent uppercase tracking-widest block","children":["ANNOTATION § 0",3]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"margin-hand text-base text-accent","children":["“","Dependencies are technical debt before they are even written.","”"]}]]}],null],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-4 border border-ink-dark/15 dark:border-paper-100/15 bg-paper-100/30 text-[10px] space-y-2","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"text-accent uppercase font-bold tracking-widest","children":"PERSONAL DISCIPLINE"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"font-sans text-ink-dark/80 dark:text-paper-200/80 leading-normal","children":"All code samples and algorithmic theorems are tested on real silicon before archiving."}]]}]]}]]}]}]]}],null],null],null]},[null,["$","$L4",null,{"parallelRouterKey":"children","segmentPath":["children","article","children","$5","children"],"error":"$undefined","errorStyles":"$undefined","errorScripts":"$undefined","template":["$","$L6",null,{}],"templateStyles":"$undefined","templateScripts":"$undefined","notFound":"$undefined","notFoundStyles":"$undefined"}]],null]},[null,["$","$L4",null,{"parallelRouterKey":"children","segmentPath":["children","article","children"],"error":"$undefined","errorStyles":"$undefined","errorScripts":"$undefined","template":["$","$L6",null,{}],"templateStyles":"$undefined","templateScripts":"$undefined","notFound":"$undefined","notFoundStyles":"$undefined"}]],null]},[[[["$","link","0",{"rel":"stylesheet","href":"/_next/static/css/bb17e40585faacf5.css","precedence":"next","crossOrigin":"$undefined"}]],["$","html",null,{"lang":"en","className":"paper-grain","children":["$","body",null,{"className":"bg-paper-50 text-ink-dark dark:bg-paper-950 dark:text-paper-50 min-h-screen selection:bg-accent selection:text-white transition-colors duration-200","children":[["$","$L7",null,{}],["$","$L8",null,{}],["$","$L9",null,{}